<ResourceDictionary x:Class="SmartCalFrames.Templates.SmartCalDataTemplates"
                    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:opts="clr-namespace:SmartCalFrames.Options"
                    xmlns:dock="clr-namespace:SmartCalFrames.Dockable"
                    xmlns:seq="clr-namespace:SmartCalFrames.Sequencer"
                    xmlns:util="clr-namespace:SmartCalFrames.Utility"
                    xmlns:nina="clr-namespace:NINA.View.Sequencer;assembly=NINA.Sequencer"
                    xmlns:mini="clr-namespace:NINA.View.Sequencer.MiniSequencer;assembly=NINA.Sequencer"
                    xmlns:ninacore="clr-namespace:NINA.Core.Utility;assembly=NINA.Core"
                    xmlns:ninactrl="clr-namespace:NINA.CustomControlLibrary;assembly=NINA.CustomControlLibrary">

    <!--
      Key naming here follows the NINA plugin template convention:
        "<PluginName>_Options"                for the Options -> Plugins page
        "<FullyQualifiedType>_Mini"            for the compact "Mini Sequencer" widget
        "<FullyQualifiedType>_Dockable"        for an imaging-tab dockable panel
      Get any of these three keys wrong and the corresponding NINA surface
      silently falls back to a default/blank template instead of throwing -
      if the options page or sequence item shows up blank, check these keys
      first.

      ROUND 41 CORRECTION - the comment above previously (and wrongly) called
      "_Mini" the key for "the compact sequencer editor row", i.e. the item's
      appearance when placed in the actual Advanced Sequencer tree. That is
      NOT what "_Mini" is for. Confirmed against the same real, published,
      working reference plugin cited elsewhere in this comment (SkyFlats by
      photon1503, SkyflatsSequenceItems/SkyflatsTemplates.xaml) plus
      NINA.Sequencer.dll's own type metadata: there are TWO separate,
      differently-keyed templates a sequence item needs, and they render in
      two different places:
        - An UNKEYED `<DataTemplate DataType="{x:Type ...}">` whose content is
          wrapped in `<nina:SequenceBlockView><nina:SequenceBlockView.SequenceItemContent>`
          - THIS is what renders inline when the item is placed and viewed in
          the actual Advanced Sequencer editor tree, which is where a user
          configuring a sequence actually looks for an item's controls.
        - A KEYED `<DataTemplate x:Key="<FullyQualifiedType>_Mini">` whose
          content is wrapped in `<mini:MiniSequenceItem><mini:MiniSequenceItem.SequenceItemContent>`
          - this renders in a separate, secondary "Mini Sequencer" widget
          (the reference plugin's own comment: "in the mini format on the
          imaging tab"). The reference plugin leaves this one nearly empty
          for both of its instructions - it is a glance/compact surface, not
          the primary editing UI.
      Round 40 defined ONLY the second one (as a bare StackPanel with no
      `MiniSequenceItem` wrapper at all, which likely broke even that
      secondary surface) and never defined the first one at all - so the
      four function checkboxes never had anywhere to render in the actual
      sequence editor tree, which is exactly the "Options do not appear" bug
      reported after Round 40. Fixed by adding the missing unkeyed
      SequenceBlockView-wrapped template below (this is the one that matters)
      and correcting the keyed _Mini/MiniSequenceItem one to match the
      reference plugin's wrapper convention. `SequenceItemContent` was
      confirmed via dump_sig.py to be a real property (getter+setter) on both
      NINA.View.Sequencer.SequenceBlockView and
      NINA.View.Sequencer.MiniSequencer.MiniSequenceItem in the installed
      NINA.Sequencer.dll.

      x:Class + the [Export(typeof(ResourceDictionary))] partial class in
      SmartCalDataTemplates.xaml.cs are both required - confirmed against a
      real, published, working NINA plugin's source (SkyFlats by
      photon1503): NINA's plugin composition discovers DataTemplates by
      finding MEF-exported ResourceDictionary instances, not by a plugin
      manually adding a resource dictionary to Application.Current at
      construction time (which is what this file's plugin used to rely on,
      before this was found and fixed - see SmartCalFramesPlugin.cs).
      Every other NINA plugin resource dictionary (Options.xaml,
      per-dockable template files, per-sequence-item template files) in
      that reference source follows this same x:Class + Export pattern.

      CRITICAL, found via a live screenshot after the plugin was already
      loading and its Options section was rendering completely empty (no
      controls at all, just a blank gap between the "Options" heading and
      "Description"): "<PluginName>" in the "_Options" key means the
      plugin's DISPLAYED NAME - i.e. AssemblyTitle, which NINA shows as the
      big heading at the top of that pane ("Smart Cal Frames", WITH a
      space, per this project's AssemblyInfo.cs) - NOT the project's
      internal namespace/AssemblyName/AssemblyProduct ("SmartCalFrames",
      no space). This key was originally written using the no-space
      internal name, so NINA's lookup for "Smart Cal Frames_Options" never
      found a match and silently rendered nothing, while the Dockable/Mini
      keys were unaffected because those use fully-qualified C# type names,
      not this display-name convention. The SkyFlats reference plugin never
      exposed this distinction because its AssemblyTitle ("SkyFlats") and
      its internal name happen to be identical (both one word, no space) -
      confirmed only once "Three Point Polar Alignment" was seen rendering
      real tabbed content in that exact same spot, proving the pane itself
      works fine and this was purely a key-string mismatch.

      A SECOND fix (removing DataType from this DataTemplate, to match two
      confirmed-working reference plugins' own source exactly) was tried and
      reverted: the very next build stopped showing up in NINA at all - no
      "Successfully loaded" log line, not even under Plugins -> Installed -
      a regression from the state before that change (which loaded fine,
      just with an empty Options pane). Restored DataType here since it's
      the one change from that round with no ruled-out mechanism, pending
      confirmation that reverting it actually restores loading. If it does,
      the empty-Options-pane problem needs a different fix than removing
      DataType - the DataContext fix below (SmartCalFramesPlugin.Instance)
      is independent of this and was kept either way.

      ROUND 64 ADDENDUM - AssemblyTitle changed again, from "Smart Cal Frames" to
      "Smart Calibration Frames" (per explicit request to rename the plugin/instruction).
      This key is exactly the failure mode described above waiting to happen a second time -
      renamed the x:Key below from "Smart Cal Frames_Options" to
      "Smart Calibration Frames_Options" in the SAME edit as the AssemblyInfo.cs AssemblyTitle
      change, specifically so the two never drift apart even for one commit. Everything else
      in this comment block above still accurately describes the ORIGINAL bug at the time it
      was found (when the title really was "Smart Cal Frames") and is left as historical
      record, not a statement of the current title.
    -->

    <!-- ROUND 59 CLEANUP - a "SmartCalBoolToVis" BooleanToVisibilityConverter used to be declared here
         too, but nothing in this file (or anywhere else in the plugin) ever referenced that key - only
         SmartCalInverseBoolToVis below is actually used (by the Flat Frames filter-picker's Visibility
         binding further down). Removed the unused declaration rather than leave a dead resource sitting
         next to the one that's real. -->
    <util:InverseBooleanToVisibilityConverter x:Key="SmartCalInverseBoolToVis"/>

    <!-- Plugin-owned icon: three stacked, OPAQUELY OCCLUDING frames (like a fanned stack of opaque
         cards, each with a rectangular window cut out), standing in for the plugin's four calibration
         frame types (flat / flat dark / bias / dark). Used in TWO places, both by this same resource key:
           - [ExportMetadata("Icon", "SCFStackedFramesSVG")] on SmartCalSequenceItem (Advanced
             Sequencer tree + Mini Sequencer widget).
           - SmartCalFramesVM's constructor sets DockableVM.ImageGeometry from this same key (Imaging
             tab's panel-toggle icon strip / the dockable panel's own tab icon).
         Declared as a GeometryGroup (not a bare PathGeometry) because ImageGeometry's real type is
         GeometryGroup (confirmed via IL dump, see SmartCalFramesVM.cs) - a PathGeometry alone fails
         that "is GeometryGroup" check silently and leaves the dockable panel with no icon even though
         the sequencer tree icon would still work fine. A GeometryGroup wrapping a single PathGeometry
         satisfies both consumers from one definition. Declared here (not borrowed from a NINA built-in
         *SVG key) so it can never silently break if NINA ever renames/removes its own internal
         resource keys. 24x24 viewbox-equivalent, single color, fills via whatever Fill brush each
         consumer's own template applies (NINA's own theme color, not something this plugin controls -
         these tree/dock icon slots are a single-Fill Geometry, no independent stroke/second color).

         EARLIER version of this shape (three hollow rings, simple boolean union) let a "behind" frame's
         solid body show through a "front" frame's own hole - physically correct for a literal see-through
         picture frame, but read as merged/see-through rather than three distinct opaque stacked frames.
         Fixed by properly z-ordering the three frames front-to-back (frame 1 = front, frame 2 = middle,
         frame 3 = back) and subtracting each frame's FULL outer footprint (not just its ring) from every
         frame behind it, so a frame in front fully occludes whatever is behind it, including through its
         own window. The resulting compound shape (4 closed loops - frame 1's full ring, plus the visible
         remainder of frame 2 and of frame 3 after subtraction) was computed numerically (rasterized mask
         + boolean occlusion + marching-squares contour extraction + Douglas-Peucker simplification, since
         hand-deriving exact rounded-rect boolean subtraction isn't practical) rather than hand-authored,
         which is why the corner coordinates below look like a dense polygon rather than clean arcs - at
         this icon's rendered size (16-32px) the polygon corners are visually indistinguishable from the
         true rounded corners they approximate. FillRule=Nonzero still set for consistency with the rest
         of this plugin's icon resources, though the generated loops also render correctly under EvenOdd
         (confirmed via side-by-side render) since marching-squares contours never self-intersect. -->
    <GeometryGroup x:Key="SCFStackedFramesSVG" FillRule="Nonzero">
        <PathGeometry FillRule="Nonzero" Figures="M1.60,0.50 L10.42,0.50 L10.94,0.71 L11.31,1.08 L11.48,1.47 L11.48,6.47 L16.32,6.49 L16.93,6.71 L17.30,7.07 L17.51,7.68 L17.53,12.52 L22.53,12.52 L22.92,12.69 L23.29,13.06 L23.50,13.58 L23.50,22.40 L23.20,23.05 L22.87,23.33 L22.40,23.50 L13.58,23.50 L13.06,23.29 L12.69,22.92 L12.52,22.53 L12.52,17.53 L7.68,17.51 L7.07,17.30 L6.71,16.93 L6.49,16.32 L6.47,11.48 L1.47,11.48 L1.08,11.31 L0.71,10.94 L0.50,10.42 L0.50,1.60 L0.80,0.95 L1.13,0.67 Z M1.60,1.06 L1.28,1.26 L1.06,1.60 L1.06,10.42 L1.15,10.59 L1.43,10.87 L1.69,10.96 L10.37,10.91 L10.63,10.83 L10.96,10.33 L10.96,1.69 L10.87,1.43 L10.42,1.06 Z M11.50,7.05 L11.48,10.50 L11.22,11.02 L10.76,11.39 L10.50,11.48 L7.05,11.50 L7.05,16.36 L7.16,16.60 L7.64,16.95 L12.50,16.95 L16.36,16.95 L16.58,16.86 L16.91,16.49 L16.95,7.64 L16.84,7.40 L16.36,7.05 Z M17.53,13.04 L17.51,16.32 L17.30,16.93 L16.93,17.30 L16.32,17.51 L13.04,17.53 L13.04,22.31 L13.13,22.57 L13.58,22.94 L18.45,22.94 L22.40,22.94 L22.66,22.81 L22.89,22.53 L22.94,13.58 L22.57,13.13 L22.31,13.04 Z" />
    </GeometryGroup>


    <!-- ROUND 41f CORRECTION - these three are NOT plugin-local converters to declare/instantiate.
         Build broke with "The tag 'DefaultBinningModesConverter' does not exist in XML namespace
         '...NINA.Core.Utility;assembly=NINA.Core'" - turns out these follow the exact same pattern as
         ProfileService (see the _Options template's ComboBox above): NINA registers ONE instance of each
         app-wide under these exact StaticResource keys (confirmed the key strings are baked into NINA's
         own compiled resources across NINA.Core.dll/NINA.WPF.Base.dll/NINA.Sequencer.dll), and the real
         SkyFlats reference plugin references them the same way - by StaticResource key only, never
         declaring a local &lt;ninacore:...Converter x:Key="..."/&gt; instance itself. Removed the three
         local declarations (xmlns:ninacore stays - still needed below for ShortRangeRule/
         ShortRangeChecker, which WERE confirmed to resolve fine via dump_sig.py) and switched every
         {StaticResource SmartCal*Converter} reference to NINA's own unprefixed key name directly. -->

    <DataTemplate x:Key="Smart Calibration Frames_Options" DataType="{x:Type opts:SmartCalOptionsVM}">
        <opts:SmartCalOptionsView/>
    </DataTemplate>

    <DataTemplate x:Key="SmartCalFrames.Dockable.SmartCalFramesVM_Dockable" DataType="{x:Type dock:SmartCalFramesVM}">
        <dock:SmartCalFramesView/>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!-- ROUND 40 built these controls; ROUND 41 fixed WHERE they render. This is the primary editing
         template - an UNKEYED DataTemplate matched by DataType, its content wrapped in
         nina:SequenceBlockView.SequenceItemContent - which is what NINA actually shows inline when the
         item is placed and viewed in the Advanced Sequencer tree. (The Round 40 version put this exact
         content in the wrong template - see the ROUND 41 CORRECTION comment above - which is why the
         item rendered as just its icon and name with no controls at all.)
         Layout: a top row of four function checkboxes (plus a fifth, dependent checkbox for Dark Frames'
         optional light-history matching), and the original Flats-specific controls below, now grayed out
         via IsEnabled when Flat Frames isn't checked (they're simply unused in that case, not removed -
         unchecking Flat Frames doesn't clear FilterName/Binning/Gain/Offset, so re-checking it later
         remembers what was there). Flat Darks/Bias/Dark Frames have no per-instance fields of their own
         here - each just runs its own tab's already-configured settings from the dockable panel, same as
         clicking that tab's button. -->
    <DataTemplate DataType="{x:Type seq:SmartCalSequenceItem}">
        <nina:SequenceBlockView>
            <nina:SequenceBlockView.SequenceItemContent>
                <!-- ROUND 41c - FontSize set here on purpose: WPF's font-related properties inherit down
                     to descendants, so setting it once at this root flows to every CheckBox/TextBlock/
                     TextBox below without repeating it on each one. Added because a live screenshot showed
                     this content's default font noticeably larger than the neighboring "Take Exposure"
                     row's text ("Time _ s | Type _ | Binning _ | Gain _ | Offset _") and other same-line
                     text in this row (Bin/Gain/Offset) - 11 is a modest reduction from WPF's 12pt default;
                     adjust here if it still doesn't visually match.
                     MUST be set as the attached property TextElement.FontSize, NOT a plain FontSize=
                     attribute - StackPanel is a Panel, not a Control, and Panel has no FontSize property
                     of its own (build error: "The property 'FontSize' does not exist in XML namespace...").
                     TextElement.FontSize is the standard WPF idiom for making font-size inheritance work
                     from a non-Control container like this one. -->
                <StackPanel TextElement.FontSize="11">
                    <!-- ROUND 41e - NINA re-styles CheckBox app-wide as an ON/OFF toggle switch that does
                         NOT display its Content text (confirmed via a live screenshot - five identical
                         unlabeled "ON"/"OFF" toggles with no visible text at all). Fixed by pairing each
                         toggle with its own separate TextBlock label - label FIRST, then the toggle it
                         controls, matching NINA's own "Take Exposure" row convention above this one
                         ("Time _ s", "Type _", etc. - label always precedes its control there). Round 41b
                         had this backwards (toggle then label); corrected per explicit feedback. -->
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Margin="0,0,0,4">
                        <TextBlock Text="Flat Frames" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,4,0"/>
                        <CheckBox IsChecked="{Binding CaptureFlats}" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,10,0"/>
                        <TextBlock Text="Flat Darks" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,4,0"/>
                        <CheckBox IsChecked="{Binding CaptureFlatDarks}" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,10,0"/>
                        <TextBlock Text="Bias" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,4,0"/>
                        <CheckBox IsChecked="{Binding CaptureBias}" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,10,0"/>
                        <TextBlock Text="Dark Frames" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,4,0"/>
                        <CheckBox IsChecked="{Binding CaptureDarkFrames}" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,10,0"/>
                        <!-- ROUND 41e - reworded per explicit direction ("Match dark exposures to light
                             frames" - clearer than "...to today's lights"). IsEnabled was previously stuck
                             permanently off/unresponsive regardless of the Dark Frames toggle - that was
                             the CaptureDarkFrames notification bug fixed in SmartCalSequenceItem.cs above,
                             not anything in this markup. -->
                        <TextBlock Text="Match dark exposures to light frames" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,4,0"
                                   IsEnabled="{Binding CaptureDarkFrames}"
                                   ToolTip="When Dark Frames is checked: for each filter, use the exposure length actually captured earlier in this sequence run instead of the Dark Frames tab's typed Exposure. Falls back to the tab's value if that filter hasn't been shot yet this run. Binning always comes from the Dark Frames tab either way."/>
                        <CheckBox IsChecked="{Binding MatchDarkExposuresToLightHistory}"
                                  IsEnabled="{Binding CaptureDarkFrames}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                  ToolTip="When Dark Frames is checked: for each filter, use the exposure length actually captured earlier in this sequence run instead of the Dark Frames tab's typed Exposure. Falls back to the tab's value if that filter hasn't been shot yet this run. Binning always comes from the Dark Frames tab either way."/>
                    </StackPanel>
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" IsEnabled="{Binding CaptureFlats}">
                        <TextBlock Text="All filters" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,4,0"/>
                        <CheckBox IsChecked="{Binding RunAllFilters}" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,10,0"/>
                        <!-- ROUND 41e - filter name is now a ComboBox of the active filter wheel's actual
                             filters instead of a free-typed name that has to match exactly. ProfileService
                             is a NINA-provided global StaticResource (confirmed present in the installed
                             NINA.WPF.Base.dll as Resources/StaticResources/ProfileService.xaml, and used
                             the identical way - Source={StaticResource ProfileService},
                             Path=ActiveProfile.FilterWheelSettings.FilterWheelFilters - by the real,
                             published SkyFlats reference plugin), so no new C# property was needed for
                             this. SelectedValue/SelectedValuePath="Name" writes the picked filter's name
                             straight into the existing FilterName string property, unchanged everywhere
                             else in this file. -->
                        <ComboBox Width="110" Margin="0,0,10,0"
                                  ItemsSource="{Binding Source={StaticResource ProfileService}, Path=ActiveProfile.FilterWheelSettings.FilterWheelFilters}"
                                  DisplayMemberPath="Name"
                                  SelectedValuePath="Name"
                                  SelectedValue="{Binding FilterName, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                  ToolTip="Filter to run Flat Frames against (ignored when 'All filters' is checked)"
                                  Visibility="{Binding RunAllFilters, Converter={StaticResource SmartCalInverseBoolToVis}}"/>
                        <!-- ROUND 41f - replaced the earlier two-dropdown Bin X / "x" / Bin Y layout (which
                             read as an unexplained extra control, and needed a typed "x" separator at all)
                             with a SINGLE Binning dropdown - matching NINA's own "Take Exposure" row
                             directly above this one exactly ("Binning 2x2", one control). Bound to the
                             connected camera's actual reported binning modes (CameraInfo.BinningModes),
                             same as that row, via the new live-CameraInfo plumbing in
                             SmartCalSequenceItem.cs. Each mode's own DisplayMemberPath="Name" already
                             renders as "1x1"/"2x2"/etc., so the "x" is part of that text, not a separate
                             floating character needing its own spacing. -->
                        <TextBlock Text="Binning" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,4,0"/>
                        <ComboBox Width="70" Margin="0,0,8,0"
                                  ItemsSource="{Binding CameraInfo.BinningModes, Converter={StaticResource DefaultBinningModesConverter}}"
                                  DisplayMemberPath="Name"
                                  SelectedItem="{Binding BinningMode, Mode=TwoWay}"/>
                        <!-- ROUND 41f - Gain/Offset now match the Take Exposure row's own presentation:
                             a HintTextBox showing the connected camera's actual default (grayed, in
                             parens - "should not be enabled if they are already set", i.e. read as "in
                             use" rather than as a live-typed value) when the field itself is empty/-1,
                             and still directly editable to override. MinusOneToEmptyStringConverter maps
                             -1 <-> "" so an untouched field reads as "use the default" both ways.
                             ShortRangeRule/ShortRangeChecker (Min -1, Max 32767) match the same
                             validation range the reference plugin's own Gain/Offset fields use. -->
                        <TextBlock Text="Gain" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,4,0"/>
                        <ninactrl:HintTextBox Width="50" Margin="0,0,8,0" VerticalAlignment="Center" HorizontalContentAlignment="Right">
                            <ninactrl:HintTextBox.HintText>
                                <Binding Path="CameraInfo.DefaultGain" Converter="{StaticResource CameraDefaultValueConverter}" Mode="OneWay"/>
                            </ninactrl:HintTextBox.HintText>
                            <ninactrl:HintTextBox.Text>
                                <Binding Path="Gain" Converter="{StaticResource MinusOneToEmptyStringConverter}" Mode="TwoWay" UpdateSourceTrigger="PropertyChanged">
                                    <Binding.ValidationRules>
                                        <ninacore:ShortRangeRule>
                                            <ninacore:ShortRangeRule.ValidRange>
                                                <ninacore:ShortRangeChecker Minimum="-1" Maximum="32767"/>
                                            </ninacore:ShortRangeRule.ValidRange>
                                        </ninacore:ShortRangeRule>
                                    </Binding.ValidationRules>
                                </Binding>
                            </ninactrl:HintTextBox.Text>
                        </ninactrl:HintTextBox>
                        <TextBlock Text="Offset" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,4,0"/>
                        <ninactrl:HintTextBox Width="50" VerticalAlignment="Center" HorizontalContentAlignment="Right">
                            <ninactrl:HintTextBox.HintText>
                                <Binding Path="CameraInfo.DefaultOffset" Converter="{StaticResource CameraDefaultValueConverter}" Mode="OneWay"/>
                            </ninactrl:HintTextBox.HintText>
                            <ninactrl:HintTextBox.Text>
                                <Binding Path="Offset" Converter="{StaticResource MinusOneToEmptyStringConverter}" Mode="TwoWay" UpdateSourceTrigger="PropertyChanged">
                                    <Binding.ValidationRules>
                                        <ninacore:ShortRangeRule>
                                            <ninacore:ShortRangeRule.ValidRange>
                                                <ninacore:ShortRangeChecker Minimum="-1" Maximum="32767"/>
                                            </ninacore:ShortRangeRule.ValidRange>
                                        </ninacore:ShortRangeRule>
                                    </Binding.ValidationRules>
                                </Binding>
                            </ninactrl:HintTextBox.Text>
                        </ninactrl:HintTextBox>
                    </StackPanel>
                </StackPanel>
            </nina:SequenceBlockView.SequenceItemContent>
        </nina:SequenceBlockView>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!-- ROUND 70 - the visual template for SmartCalSequenceItem.PauseForCoverSwapAsync's dialog content
         (seq:CoverSwapDialogResult - see that class's own doc comment for why this plugin uses its own
         type/template here instead of NINA's own MessageBoxResult). Confirmed via a targeted dnfile IL
         disassembly of the installed NINA.Core.dll's WindowService.GenerateWindow that the dialog Window
         uses SizeToContent (plus a MinWidth/MinHeight floor it applies internally, not something this
         plugin can change) rather than a fixed size - so this template's own explicit root Width is what
         actually controls the rendered dialog's width, and keeping the layout compact (no extra vertical
         padding beyond what's needed to read the message and reach the buttons) is what controls its
         height. Background color deliberately matches the dockable panel's own pause banner
         (SmartCalFramesView.xaml) per explicit feedback after real testing - both are changed together
         so they can't drift apart. ROUND 71 - changed from the original lighter red (#FFEF9A9A) to a
         darker, fully saturated red (#FFFF0000) per a follow-up screenshot of the exact shade wanted.
         Buttons have no local Style, so they keep NINA's ambient button chrome automatically.
         Restored here after being lost from an earlier snapshot of this file. -->
    <DataTemplate DataType="{x:Type seq:CoverSwapDialogResult}">
        <Border Background="#FFFF0000" Padding="16" Width="440">
            <StackPanel>
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Message}" TextWrapping="Wrap" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="Black"
                           Margin="0,0,0,12"/>
                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Right">
                    <Button Content="Continue" Command="{Binding ContinueCommand}" FontWeight="Bold"
                            Padding="14,4" MinWidth="80" Margin="0,0,8,0"/>
                    <Button Content="Cancel" Command="{Binding CancelCommand}" Padding="14,4" MinWidth="80"/>
                </StackPanel>
            </StackPanel>
        </Border>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!-- ROUND 41 - this is the SECONDARY "Mini Sequencer" widget template (a different, glance-only
         surface, not the Advanced Sequencer tree above). Content wrapped in
         mini:MiniSequenceItem.SequenceItemContent per the reference plugin's convention. Kept
         deliberately minimal/read-only here (a single line via the item's own ToString(), which already
         lists which functions are enabled) rather than duplicating live-editable checkboxes in two
         places at once - the reference plugin (SkyFlats) leaves this surface empty entirely for the same
         reason: it's a compact status glance, not a second copy of the editor. -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="SmartCalFrames.Sequencer.SmartCalSequenceItem_Mini" DataType="{x:Type seq:SmartCalSequenceItem}">
        <mini:MiniSequenceItem>
            <mini:MiniSequenceItem.SequenceItemContent>
                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                </StackPanel>
            </mini:MiniSequenceItem.SequenceItemContent>
        </mini:MiniSequenceItem>
    </DataTemplate>

</ResourceDictionary>
